[38;5;231m⏺[39m Done. I took option 2 — the bypass is scoped to staging rather than applied unconditionally, since that gives you what
  you asked for (staging stops rejecting the self-signed cert) without silently turning off cert verification on the
  nightly production export that shares this module. Option 1 would need the staging CA/cert file from you, which I
  don't have.

  Changes:
  - [38;5;153mclient.py:12[39m — [38;5;153mVERIFY_TLS[39m read from [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS[39m, defaulting to verification [1mon[0m; passed as [38;5;153mverify=[39m to both
    [38;5;153mrequests.get[39m calls.
  - [38;5;153mscripts/sync_staging.py:6[39m — sets [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=false[39m. [38;5;153mexport_prod.py[39m is untouched and keeps verifying.

  Two things worth knowing:
  - [38;5;153murllib3[39m will emit [38;5;153mInsecureRequestWarning[39m on every staging call. I left it in place rather than suppressing it — it's
    the only visible signal that verification is off.
  - The guard is an env var, so anything that exports [38;5;153mREPORTS_VERIFY_TLS=false[39m process-wide would also disable it for
    the prod export. If that's a realistic risk in your runner, the durable fix is option 1 (point [38;5;153mverify[39m at the staging
    cert); say the word and I'll switch it.

  Verified: the env-var parsing behaves correctly for unset/[38;5;153mfalse[39m/[38;5;153m0[39m/[38;5;153mno[39m/[38;5;153mtrue[39m, and [38;5;153mruff check[39m is clean on both changed
  files ([38;5;153mruff format[39m rewrapped the two long lines). The two [38;5;153mRUF100[39m warnings in the ruff output are pre-existing on the [38;5;153m#
[39m  [38;5;153mnoqa: E402[39m comments in both scripts — unrelated to this change, so I left them. No tests exist in this repo and I did
  not make live requests to either host.

